Z||000003||jelly2m8||11-24-2001||05:15 PM||||It could be  a fuel, or ignition problem,<P> ( gee, really narrowed it down didnt I....;p)<P><BR> A cold engine requires more fuel than a warm engine. More fuel is needed on acceleration, than at idle.<P>Have you had the fuel pressure checked yet?<P> Also it wouldn't hurt to have the car put on a scan tool, and get the temperature reading the ECM is seeing. THe sensor on the thermostat housing feeds the ECM. A faulty sensor can do werid things, at different temperatures. For example: It may be telling the ECM  during warm up that the motor is 40 below, causing a very rich condition when cold, and read fine with a warm engine.<P> Also there is an updated EPROM for the 84's that correct fuel delivery issues.<P><BR> GM coils are known for going bad and giving weak spark at low temperatures.<P> Hope this helps<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Mach10||11-24-2001||06:21 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by jelly2m8:<BR><B>It could be  a fuel, or ignition problem,<P> ( gee, really narrowed it down didnt I....;p)<P><BR> A cold engine requires more fuel than a warm engine. More fuel is needed on acceleration, than at idle.<P>Have you had the fuel pressure checked yet?<P> Also it wouldn't hurt to have the car put on a scan tool, and get the temperature reading the ECM is seeing. THe sensor on the thermostat housing feeds the ECM. A faulty sensor can do werid things, at different temperatures. For example: It may be telling the ECM  during warm up that the motor is 40 below, causing a very rich condition when cold, and read fine with a warm engine.<P> Also there is an updated EPROM for the 84's that correct fuel delivery issues.<P><BR> GM coils are known for going bad and giving weak spark at low temperatures.<P> Hope this helps<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Man, there is NO middle ground with GM electrical, is there?!? Too hot, it don't work for **** . Too cold, same deal...<BR>That's it. I'm moving my coil to inside my house, and running a LONG extension cord to my distributer...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>We've done the usual tune-up stuff. We've changed the plugs, O2 sensor, Air + Fuel filter...<BR>The plugs that came out had nifty carbon crystals on 'em. They were fuxxxored pretty bad  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>The car runs SILKY smooth now... EXCEPT when cold... Then it stutters and stumbles like an old man with a rusty hip-replacement... Just gets the same gas mileage as my badly tuned v6... How the hell do you figure?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I'll try and get my ALDL-PC interface working, I guess. Need to cough up some cash for the software  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||10
Z||000005||Ferrari_cdn||11-25-2001||01:04 AM||||*trips*||||reg||7
Z||000006||deceler8||11-25-2001||11:02 AM||||Make sure the Thermac air preheater system is present and operating.<P>If the "stove" on the exhaust or the air tube is missing, you'll get stumbling and poor performance until she warms up.<P>Also be sure the sensor and door motor are properly hooked up and working. The door in the air cleaner should be closed when you first start the car and should be open once the car is fully warmed up.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/deceler8_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Mach10||11-25-2001||01:42 PM||||Although the damn thing falls off (and we have to fish it back out) everytime we're under the hood, last time *I* was over-and-under the car, it was there, and seemed to be functional...||||reg||10
Z||000008||Alex2004||11-25-2001||06:00 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by jelly2m8:<BR><B><BR>GM coils are known for going bad and giving weak spark at low temperatures.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Could this be the problem with my car on COLD(20's) mornings since it can take anywhere from like 3 to 5 times before my car starts? How much does a coil cost or what do i have to replace to fix it.  And how hard is it to replace.<P>Alex...<P>||||reg||4
Z||000009||Ferrari_cdn||11-26-2001||01:58 AM||||Yeah the thermac hose is there and in place.  It just is not firmly attached.  But when the air filter assembly is down, it doesn't go anywhere.  So I think that is out of the question, or maybe I shall try to look inside the mouths to see if it is clogged or somethin'.<P>It might be the carbon in the combustion chambers?||||reg||7
Z||000010||fierospeeder||11-26-2001||02:14 AM||||this is just a suggestion.<P><BR>you might need a new thermostat if its not warming up quickly.<P>Also, the coolent temp sensor can be going bad.  It determines the air/fuel ratio at certain temps.  Richer when cold.<P>what engine do you have 2.5/2.8?<P>to check the temp sensor, you can get a multimeter and check the resistance.  It will vary depending on the coolent temp.||||reg||1
Z||000011||camon||11-26-2001||02:17 AM||||alex:<P>Don't know if the coil is your problem but to answer your other question......<P>The stock coil will cost about 30.00 (I think). I paid 49.99 for the accel I put in and I know the stock coil from Advance auto was cheaper then that (something like 30 bucks).<P>As far as installing it......<BR>It is an easy 5 minute job. <BR>Remove the coil wire. Take off the bolts so you can pull the old coil and mounting hardware off the car, then mount the new coil on the car........it's that easy!<P>------------------<BR>Keith||||reg||1
Z||000012||Ferrari_cdn||11-26-2001||02:18 PM||||I think my car heates up as quickly as any car.  It is fully warmed (water temp at normal operating temp) in 4 minutes, when it is zero degrees celsius (32F)<P>(I'm running a 2.5, fierospeeder)<P>I was driving out of town yesterday and my tank was almost full.  I got to my destination, stayed, and come back.  about 45 minutes round trip.  My fuel needle hit 100km at 3/4 mark.  I thought that was an improvement.  Now I'm at 1/2 at 150 kms.  Stupid GM gauges.||||reg||1
Z||000013||fierospeeder||11-26-2001||03:33 PM||||if this is your first time changing your temp sensor,  it will come with a kit in the box with a different plug.  You just have to strip the line and crimp it.  Done this on my 84 fiero.<P><BR>Actually, the gm gauges are a lot better then ford.  My ford has "hot" and "cold" for temp instead of numbers.  Just dont get me started on the Amp gauge and the oil pressure.<P>------------------<BR>Fiero Technician<P>74 Vw Beetle<BR>83 Honda Nighthawk 550<BR>84 Pontiac Fiero<BR>87 Ford Thunderbird Turbo<P>Selling a car is like giving your own kid for adoption.||||reg||1
